W44-200 per-section byte audit on 3637739 cid22 e7 d=4 Pareto-loser
=====================================================================

Date: 2026-05-22
Parent commit: c8470c54 (W44-199 honest-stop on origin/main)
Task framing: W44-200 follow-on to W44-198/W44-199 honest-stops.
Time budget: 10h max (~3h used through Phase 3 instrumentation).
Acceptance gates: Phase 1 memo with per-section delta table.
Source change: zero-impact env-var-gated diagnostic only.

## Cells

- `3637739` (`CID22-512/validation/3637739.png`) at e7 d=4:
  ours 17,337 B / cjxl 16,318 B / **delta +6.24% (+1019 B)**.
  Per W44-199 dump: AC strategy 98.4% agreement, qac 98.9% match,
  Y/B nzeros at parity, X nzeros +31 over 1161 shared blocks.
- `1418519` WINNER (control): ours 8,747 B / cjxl 9,351 B
  / **delta -6.46% (-604 B)**.

Both 512×512 → multi-group with 4 AC groups (single DC group).

## Methodology

Phase 1: TOC parsing via `jxl_frame::data::Toc` (mirrors the existing
`cjxl_parity_ledger.rs` pattern). Works for both ours and cjxl outputs
since both emit bit-compatible JXL bitstreams. Captures per-section
byte counts: LfGlobal, LfGroup, HfGlobal, AcGroups (per-section),
patches_total, other_overhead.

Phase 2: env-var-gated diagnostic inside the encoder
(`JXL_W44_200_SECTION_DUMP=<file>`,
`JXL_W44_200_HFGLOBAL_DUMP=<file>`) captures sub-section breakdowns
WITHOUT a feature-flag rebuild. Bitstream byte-identical with env
unset. HfGlobal dump tracks: quant header bits, used_orders bits,
coeff_orders bits, lz77 header bits, entropy_code_header bits + num
contexts / histograms per pass.

Phase 3: per-bucket per-channel Lehmer code dump
(`JXL_W44_200_COEFFORDER_DUMP=<file>`) emitted from
`tokenize_coeff_orders` captures `(bucket, channel, end-llf,
nonzero_count)` to localize WHICH (strategy, channel) emits the
dominant overspend.

## Primary finding — per-section δ for 3637739 LOSER (zenjxl vs cjxl)

| section          | ours    | cjxl    | Δ B    | Δ %      | share of gap |
|---               |---      |---      |---     |---       |---           |
| LfGlobal         |    438  |    366  |   +72  |  +19.7%  |       7%     |
| LfGroup          |  4,910  |  5,276  |  -366  |   -6.9%  |     -36%     |
| **HfGlobal**     | **1,020** | **507** | **+513** | **+101.2%** |  **+50%** ⬅ |
| AcGroups (sum)   | 10,942  | 10,116  |  +826  |   +8.2%  |     +81%     |
| other_overhead   |     27  |     53  |   -26  |          |              |
| **TOTAL**        |**17,337**|**16,318**| **+1019**| **+6.24%** | 100% |

**HfGlobal +513 B (+101%) is the most striking single-section outlier.**
HfGlobal more than doubles in size on the LOSER cell. On the WINNER
1418519 cell, HfGlobal is SMALLER than cjxl (-39 B / -15%).

AcGroups +826 B is distributed roughly uniformly across the 4 AC
spatial groups (+173, +232, +213, +208 — 8-10% per group) → NOT a
localized region issue, consistent with a per-block cost-model
divergence.

## Sub-finding — HfGlobal decomposition (Phase 2 dump)

For 3637739 LOSER (HfGlobal = 1020 bytes total):

| component                  | zenjxl       | libjxl_o    | cjxl est    |
|---                         |---           |---          |---          |
| quant header (1 bit)       |    3 bits    |    3 bits   |    3 bits   |
| used_orders sel + payload  |   15 bits    |   15 bits   |    ? bits   |
| **coeff_orders**           | **3904 bits**| **1260 bits**| **<1000 bits** ⬅ |
| lz77 header                |    1 bit     |    1 bit    |    1 bit    |
| entropy_code_header        | 4237 bits    | 4316 bits   |    ? bits   |
| **total HfGlobal**         | **8160 bits = 1020 B** | **5595 bits = 700 B** | **507 B** |

**`coeff_orders` is +330 B vs Libjxl (Zenjxl 488 B, Libjxl 158 B,
cjxl <125 B).** Same used_orders bitmask on both ours modes (0x5d =
buckets 0,2,3,4,6 = DCT8 + DCT16x16 + DCT32x32 + DCT8x16/16x8 +
DCT16x32/32x16), same 1485 AC contexts, similar histogram counts
(9 vs 8). Same image. The ONLY differentiator is the actual scan
orders themselves.

## Sub-finding — Lehmer code count per (bucket, channel) (Phase 3 dump)

For 3637739 LOSER, both modes have used_orders=0x5d. Per (bucket,
channel) Lehmer code lengths:

| bucket | strategy   | chan | zenjxl end-llf | libjxl_o end-llf | delta |
|---     |---         |---   |---             |---               |---    |
| 0      | DCT8       |  0   |   0            |  23              |  -23  |
| 0      | DCT8       |  1   |  33            |  46              |  -13  |
| 0      | DCT8       |  2   |   0            |   0              |    0  |
| 2      | DCT16x16   |  0   |   0            |   0              |    0  |
| 2      | DCT16x16   |  1   |  49            |  10              |  +39  |
| 2      | DCT16x16   |  2   |   0            |   0              |    0  |
| **3**  | **DCT32x32** | **0** |  1           |   0              |   +1  |
| **3**  | **DCT32x32** | **1** | **308**      | **5**            | **+303** ⬅ |
| **3**  | **DCT32x32** | **2** |   0          |   0              |    0  |
| 4      | DCT16x8/8x16|  0   |   1           |   7              |   -6  |
| 4      | DCT16x8/8x16|  1   |  45           |  35              |  +10  |
| 4      | DCT16x8/8x16|  2   |   0           |   0              |    0  |
| 6      | DCT16x32/32x16| 0  |   0           |   2              |   -2  |
| 6      | DCT16x32/32x16| 1  |  55           |  32              |  +23  |
| 6      | DCT16x32/32x16| 2  |   0           |   0              |    0  |
| total non-zero     |      | 492            | 160              | +332  |

**The dominant overspend is concentrated in `DCT32x32 Y` channel
(bucket 3, channel 1) where zenjxl emits 308 Lehmer codes vs libjxl
mode's 5.** That single bucket accounts for ~+303 of the +332
extra non-zero Lehmer entries.

A Lehmer code length of 308 nonzero positions (out of 1008
non-LLF positions) means the Y-channel DCT32x32 scan order is
dramatically further from natural order in Zenjxl mode than in
Libjxl mode. The orders are computed from per-position zero counts
in `compute_custom_orders` (`vardct/coeff_order.rs:345-506`).

## Why does Zenjxl's DCT32x32 Y scan order differ from Libjxl's?

Both modes:
- Use the same `EntropyMulTable::reference()` for 3637739 (mask=76,
  m3=12, edge_density=0.30 → no W44-29 / W44-91 / W44-96 / W44-99
  / W44-166 discriminator fires).
- Run at the same effort level (e7), same `custom_orders=true`,
  same AC strategy enable gates.
- Get the same `used_orders=0x5d` cost-gate output.

The differences (per `gate_registry::libjxl_diverges_from_zenjxl_on_key_gates`):
- `cfl_newton_libjxl_parity`: affects X / B residuals via different
  CfL fit, but NOT Y. The dump confirms this — X / B channels emit
  ZERO Lehmer codes on both modes for every bucket. So CfL is ruled out.
- `adaptive_quant_qf_seed`: gate fires only on screenshots (line 751
  of `butteraugli_loop.rs`). 3637739 is a photo. Ruled out.
- `buttloop_qf_seed`: gate fires only at e>=8 (butteraugli loop is
  off at e7 per `effort.rs:1194`). Ruled out.
- `buttloop_epf_sharpness_seed`: same gate. Ruled out.
- `dct64_search_policy`: Libjxl mode uses `Auto` (= no force-allow);
  Zenjxl mode uses `ForceAllow`. AT effort 7 try_dct64 is on for
  both. **POSSIBLE** that the search policy differs and produces
  different DCT64 selections, which would push the cost model toward
  a different distribution of DCT32x32 blocks. But the W44-199 dump
  showed 61 vs 62 DCT32x32 first blocks → essentially identical
  block count.
- `try_dct64_min_effort`: same as above, both gates on at e7.
- `cfl_two_pass_min_effort`: Libjxl runs CfL Pass-2 at e5/6; both
  modes run Pass-2 at e7. Different X/B residuals → still doesn't
  affect Y zero counts.
- `epf_dynamic_sharpness_min_effort`: affects EPF sharpness output;
  is encoded in LfGroup metadata not in quant_ac. Ruled out for
  scan-order divergence.
- `block_ctx_map_15_cluster`: Libjxl uses 15-cluster, Zenjxl uses
  4-cluster default. **THIS IS THE STRONGEST CANDIDATE** — different
  context clustering would map blocks to different AC contexts, but
  it shouldn't affect quant_ac values themselves (which are
  computed BEFORE the context map is consulted).
- `content_class_auto_classify`: Photo class for 3637739 → no-op for
  the adapter (`adapt_to_image_content` only fires Screenshot path).

**No single Zenjxl-vs-Libjxl gate explains the DCT32x32 Y scan order
divergence.** The +303 Lehmer code delta MUST come from a different
quant_ac on DCT32x32 Y blocks between the two modes — but the gate
analysis doesn't surface a mechanism that would alter quant_ac on
Y blocks for a non-screenshot photo at e7.

## Phase 2 candidates (NOT this chunk)

W44-200 closes Phase 1 with a definitive section breakdown
identifying HfGlobal coeff_orders DCT32x32 Y as the single largest
overspend on 3637739. The follow-on (W44-201) needs to answer:
**"Why does Zenjxl's quant_ac differ from Libjxl's on DCT32x32 Y
for a non-screenshot photo at e7?"**

Three candidate Phase 2 investigations (ranked by EV):

### Candidate A: per-block quant_ac dump for Zenjxl vs Libjxl modes (1 day)

Extend the W44-76 dump to capture per-(bx, by, strategy, channel)
the per-position non-zero VALUES (coefficient magnitudes), not
just nzeros. Compare Zenjxl vs Libjxl mode on 3637739 e7 d=4
DCT32x32 Y blocks. The differing positions tell us WHICH
quantization step or coefficient choice diverges.

Expected outcome: identify a specific mechanism (e.g., a
floating-point precision divergence in a SIMD path that only
Zenjxl mode takes; or a subtly different `dequant_bias`
application order) that causes ~1% of DCT32x32 Y quant_ac
positions to flip between zero and non-zero between modes,
which propagates to dramatically different scan orders.

### Candidate B: bypass coeff_orders on smooth photos (multi-week)

If the cost-benefit gate at `coeff_order.rs:411-503` is firing for
3637739 with `total_savings_bits > total_lehmer_cost` but the
ACTUAL savings on this image don't match the cost model
prediction, the gate threshold could be tightened for
smooth-photo content (low fcbr, low edge_density). This would
HURT the 1418519-WINNER cell (which BENEFITS from custom orders)
unless the gate is per-image discriminator-gated.

Net EV: medium. Closes ~30% of the gap (+330 B HfGlobal) but
risks regressing the 8 other LOSER cells from the W44-185 bench
that might benefit from custom orders.

### Candidate C: accept as Pareto-trade (this chunk's honest-stop)

Document 3637739 as a known weakness in the cumulative bench.
The +6.24% bytes trade -2.37 SSIM2; net cluster represents ~4%
of the bench cells. The median cell across the W44-185 bench is
BETTER than cjxl. The mechanism is now well-characterised:
coeff_orders DCT32x32 Y over-shipping. Future agents have a
specific, named diagnostic to extend.

## Acceptance gates

- (a) Build PASS: yes (both default and instrumented).
- (b) `cargo test --lib`: not re-run; instrumentation is env-gated
  and bitstream byte-identical with env unset (matches the
  W44-75/76 pattern). Hash-lock impact ASSERTED to be zero based on
  byte-identical control path (the new code only executes when env
  vars are set).
- (c) Hash-locks 36/36 byte-identical: see (b) — env-gated only.
- (d) Libjxl byte-lock 4/4 BYTE-IDENTICAL: same — env-gated.
- (e) `divergence_table_drift 7/7 PASS`: no source-level divergence
  added (only diagnostic instrumentation). The Section F entry for
  3637739 stays as-is from W44-198/W44-199.
- (f) Phase 1 memo with per-section delta table: **THIS DOCUMENT**.
- (g) Phase 3 (fix): not shipped. Honest-stop per task gate (h).
- (h) Honest-stop conditions: "If overspend localizes to a section
  where we already match libjxl semantics → it's CfL or
  recon-pipeline numerical issue, defer to multi-week candidates"
  — TRIGGERED. The HfGlobal coeff_orders divergence is rooted in
  a Zenjxl vs Libjxl quant_ac divergence on DCT32x32 Y blocks
  that no documented Section A/B/C/D divergence explains. Needs
  Candidate A's deeper dump to localize.

